course: biology
lesson: 248
title: Defences: skunks, porcupines, hedgehogs and armour
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An animal that cannot run fast can still be very hard to eat: it can be spiky, smelly, or covered in armour like a knight.
explain: A hedgehog rolls into a tight ball of spines, a pangolin curls up in scales so tough that a lion cannot bite through, and a tortoise pulls its head into a shell that is part of its own skeleton.
explain: A skunk does not spray at once: first it stamps, hisses and lifts its tail, a warning that says «last chance».
explain: Some defences sound like cartoons but are real: a beetle that fires boiling chemicals from its bottom with a pop, and a lizard that squirts blood from its eyes.
explain: Cartoons get some of it wrong, though: porcupines cannot shoot their quills, and a tortoise can never climb out of its shell.
fact: Skunks spray a very smelly oily liquid from glands under the tail at an attacker's face, but first warn by stamping, hissing and raising the tail, and spotted skunks even do a handstand; the smell can carry over a kilometre, and tomato juice does not really wash it off.
fact: Porcupines cannot shoot their quills; the quills come out easily when touched, have tiny barbs that work into an attacker's skin, and grow back; a North American porcupine may have about 30,000.
fact: A hedgehog has about 5,000 to 7,000 spines, which are hollow hairs without barbs, and strong muscles to curl up tight, but badgers are strong enough to unroll one.
fact: Armadillo armour is made of bone plates covered with tough skin; only the three-banded armadillo can roll into a full ball, and the nine-banded armadillo almost always has four identical babies.
fact: Pangolins are mammals covered in scales made of keratin, like your nails, and roll up so tight that a lion cannot bite through; they are among the most trafficked mammals because some people wrongly think the scales are medicine.
fact: A tortoise's shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its backbone and ribs, so it can never climb out; some tortoises live over 150 years, and Jonathan on Saint Helena was reported to be born around 1832.
fact: Sea urchins are covered with movable spines that point towards a threat, and their mouth underneath has five teeth called Aristotle's lantern.
fact: Stink bugs release bad-smelling chemicals when disturbed, and smells work well as defences because predators learn quickly.
fact: The bombardier beetle mixes two chemicals in a chamber in its bottom, they react with a burst of heat, and it fires a boiling hot spray with a pop in almost any direction.
fact: The horned lizard squirts blood from its eyes, sometimes over a metre, at dogs and coyotes; the blood tastes nasty to dogs but not to birds, so it uses this only against dog-like enemies.
fact: Crabs raise their claws to look bigger, male fiddler crabs wave one giant claw, and many crabs can grow back a lost claw over several moults.
exercise: What does a skunk do before it sprays? | Stamps, hisses and raises its tail as a warning | Nothing at all, it just turns round and sprays at once | Climbs the nearest tree and hides | Rolls into a spiky ball
exercise: How far can the smell of a skunk's spray carry? | Over a kilometre | Only a few centimetres | About one metre | Round the whole world
exercise: Can a porcupine shoot its quills? | No, they come out easily when touched | Yes, it fires them like arrows from a bow | Yes, but only at lions and tigers | Only when it sneezes hard
exercise: Why are porcupine quills so hard to pull out? | They have tiny barbs | They are glued with spit | They are made of steel | They are very hot to touch
exercise: About how many spines does a hedgehog have? | About 5,000 to 7,000 | About 50 | About 500,000 | Exactly 100
exercise: Which animal is strong enough to unroll a hedgehog? | A badger | A field mouse | A sparrow | A pond frog
exercise: What is armadillo armour made of? | Bone plates covered with tough skin | Hard tree bark glued on with spit | Metal from rocks | Thick mud that dries and freezes solid
exercise: Which armadillo can roll into a full ball? | The three-banded armadillo | Every armadillo | The nine-banded armadillo | No armadillo at all
exercise: What are pangolin scales made of? | Keratin, like your nails | Bone, like your skull | Wood, like a tree | Shell, like a snail
exercise: Why are pangolins taken illegally from the wild? | Some people wrongly think their scales are medicine | Their scales are made of real gold and silver | They are kept as guard dogs on farms in the mountains | They are needed to dig mines
exercise: Can a tortoise climb out of its shell? | No, the shell is part of its skeleton | Yes, when it gets far too hot in the summer | Yes, to change into a bigger one as it grows | Only at night, when nobody is there to see it
exercise: How do sea urchin spines help it? | They move and point towards a threat | They pump water round through its body | They help it fly | They catch the sun for food, like leaves
exercise: Why do smells work well as a defence? | Predators learn quickly to avoid them | Smells make the animal invisible to its enemies | Smells put predators to sleep for hours | Smells make the prey taste sweet and nice
exercise: How does a bombardier beetle make its spray hot? | Two chemicals mix and react with a burst of heat | It warms the spray in the sun first | It rubs its back legs together very fast to make the heat | It drinks hot water first
exercise: Who does the horned lizard squirt blood at? | Dogs and coyotes | Birds and bats at night | Ants and little beetles | Fish and frogs in ponds
exercise: Why does a crab hold up its claws at you? | To look bigger and threatening | To wave hello to you in a friendly way | To catch the warm sunshine on its claws | To show it is hungry for people
test: In a Tom and Jerry style cartoon, a porcupine shoots quills like arrows and Tom gets stuck full of them from far away. What is right? | Porcupines cannot shoot quills, they come out when touched | Porcupines can fire their quills more than a hundred metres | Porcupines shoot quills only at cats | Porcupines have no quills at all
test: In a cartoon a skunk lifts its tail and everyone faints at once. What does a real skunk do first? | Warns by stamping, hissing and raising its tail | Faints at once | Hugs its enemy tightly until it gives up and goes | Bites with venom fangs
test: In a cartoon a tortoise jumps out of its shell and runs off in its pants. What is true? | A tortoise can never leave its shell, it is part of its skeleton | Tortoises swap their old shells for bigger ones every single spring | Old tortoises leave their shells behind when it is time to die | Tortoises keep spare shells at home
test: «Sonic rolls into a ball and smashes stuff, real hedgehogs do that right» What is right? | They roll into a ball to protect themselves, not to attack | Yes, they roll at their enemies at high speed, like spiky bowling balls | Yes, they smash rocks by rolling | No, hedgehogs cannot roll up
test: «dog got sprayed by a skunk, dump tomato juice on him» What is true? | Tomato juice does not really wash the smell off | Tomato juice removes all of the smell at once, after one bath | Tomato juice makes it smell of roses | Tomato juice makes the dog spray back
test: «3 crabs on the beach, no wait 4. how many claws» | 8 | 6 | 12 | 40
test: «2 hedgehogs and 3 tortoises. legs?» | 20 | 10 | 15 | 24
test: What would happen if a horned lizard squirted blood at a hawk? | Not much, the blood tastes nasty to dogs but not to birds | The hawk would fall asleep straight away | The hawk would turn blue | The hawk would be blinded for life by the blood in its eyes
test: «my mum reckons that pangolin is a pine cone with legs lol» What is it really? | A mammal with keratin scales that rolls up in danger | A walking plant with green leaves | A kind of lizard with feathers that lives in the trees of Africa | A baby armadillo
test: «picked up a bug and now my fingers stink, gross» What was it probably? | A stink bug defending itself with smelly chemicals | A bee making honey in its hive | A ladybird laying eggs | A spider spinning a web between the leaves
test: A crab loses a claw in a fight. What can happen? | Many crabs grow it back over several moults | It stays without a claw for ever | It grows two claws in its place at once | It turns into a different animal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the defensive conduct of the porcupine? | Its barbed quills detach readily upon contact but cannot be projected | It discharges its quills over considerable distances, much as an archer looses arrows | It possesses venomous quills in the manner of a scorpion | It relies exclusively upon flight from its pursuers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a tortoise is incapable of vacating its shell. | The shell forms part of its skeleton, being joined to the backbone and ribs | The shell is fastened by a lock of hardened mud | The tortoise is too large to pass through the openings | The shell is cemented to the skin by saliva which sets as hard as stone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the defence of the bombardier beetle. | Two chemicals are combined within a chamber and react to discharge a boiling spray | It emits a beam of light which dazzles its attackers | It feigns death for a period of several hours | It stings with a venomous appendage upon its head, injecting a paralysing fluid
exam: Ascertain the conduct of the skunk prior to the discharge of its spray. | It issues warnings by stamping, hissing and the raising of its tail | It discharges its spray without any prior warning whatsoever, even at the faintest sound | It retreats into a burrow and seals the entrance | It summons other skunks by a loud call
